  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "If \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are the roots of \\(x^2+mx+n=0,\\) \\(m\\ne0,\\) \\(n\\ne0,\\) then the sum of the roots is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac12\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac12\\)",
    "D": "\\(1\\)",
    "E": "undetermined"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Because \\(n\\) is itself a root, substituting \\(x=n\\) gives an equation containing the sum we want:\n\\[\nn^2+mn+n=0.\n\\]\nFactor out \\(n\\):\n\\[\nn(n+m+1)=0.\n\\]\nSince \\(n\\ne0\\), division by \\(n\\) is valid, giving \\(n+m+1=0\\). Thus the sum of the roots is \\(m+n=-1\\).\n\nThe conditions are consistent: \\(m=1\\) and \\(n=-2\\) give \\(x^2+x-2=(x-1)(x+2)\\), whose roots are precisely \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\). The requested sum is therefore \\(\\boxed{-1}\\), choice B.",
